<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bleiburg repatriations</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bleiburg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">repatriations</a> took place in May 1945, when British forces in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> refused the surrender of retreating soldiers of the Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and accompanying civilians near the town of Bleiburg and handed them over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-partisans">Yugoslav Partisans</a>. Many tens of thousands were killed in mass executions or died on forced marches. The events remain a sensitive subject in Croatian memory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bleiburg-repatriations.jpg" alt="Bleiburg repatriations" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ratni_zarobljenici_kod_Bleiburga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> was a fascist puppet state established in German-occupied Yugoslavia, and it carried out genocidal campaigns against its minority populations. By the end of 1944 the Partisans, with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, had established control in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, Montenegro and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, and the collapse of the Syrmian front in April 1945 hastened the German withdrawal from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>.</p><p>On 3 May 1945 the government of the Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> decided to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and the Croatian Armed Forces were ordered to move there to surrender to British forces. The Slovene National Council issued similar orders the same day. The soldiers were accompanied by civilians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Slovene-led columns fought their way to the Austrian border near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klagenfurt">Klagenfurt</a> on 14 May. The British accepted their surrender and interned them in the nearby Viktring camp. On 15 May the head of the main Croatian column, some 25,000 to 30,000 people, reached the field at Bleiburg. Negotiations were held in Bleiburg Castle, the British negotiator being Brigadier Thomas Scott. The British refused to accept the surrender and directed the Croatian forces to surrender to the Partisans, which their leadership did.</p><p>Other prisoners in British captivity were repatriated in the following weeks, from camps such as Viktring and Krumpendorf, from 18 May. The British cancelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">repatriations</a> on 31 May after reports of massacres. The Yugoslav authorities moved the prisoners on forced marches to internment and labour camps, and those who lagged behind were shot. The largest mass executions took place at Tezno, estimated at 15,000 Croatian prisoners of war, and at Kočevski Rog, Huda Jama and Macelj.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Under communist rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> the killings were a taboo subject, and information about them was suppressed. Mass graves were concealed or destroyed in accordance with an order of 18 May 1945. Investigation of the graves began only in the 1990s, when 1,163 bodies were excavated from 23 mass graves at Macelj in 1992.</p><p>Historians have been unable to establish the number of victims. Estimates given in the literature range from 50,000 to 200-250,000, while others cite 20,000 to 40,000, noting that pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> writers inflate the figures. Most victims were members of Croatian, Slovene, Montenegrin and Serb collaborationist forces, and civilians were also among the dead. Since the breakup of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> an annual commemoration has been held at Bleiburg, and it has drawn controversy because of Ustaše symbols displayed there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bleiburg repatriations” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its troops and civilians fleeing to Austria were turned back at <b>Bleiburg</b> and many were killed by the Partisans.</blockquote><a href="/state/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia</a></li></ul></section>
